  "brief": "Translate the given UI strings from English into zh for a software product.\nThese are short UI microcopy strings - button labels, headings, aria-labels, and one-line descriptions - in a design-tool web app called Lolly. Keep them as short as the source; this is UI chrome, not prose.\nRegister: plain, no honorifics.\nNever translate these terms - copy them verbatim wherever they appear: \"Lolly\", \"SUSE\", \"Geeko\", \"Content Credentials\", \"C2PA\", \"Pose Geeko\", \"Neurospicy\".\nPreserve every {placeholder} token exactly (same braces, same name, same count) - these are runtime interpolations, not prose.\nPreserve any inline HTML exactly: the same tags, the same count, every attribute (href, target, rel) byte-for-byte, and every HTML entity (&amp;, &lt;). Translate only the human text around and between the tags - never a tag name, an attribute value, or the contents of a <code> element.\nPreserve punctuation choices like → and & as-is where they read naturally in the target language; do not add explanatory text.\nMatch the source length and register. Do not pad or embellish.\nTerm-sense guidance - translate each of these by its stated sense, NEVER literally: \"house style\" = the organisation's OWN editorial/design standard, as in a publishing or fashion house - never a residence or home decor. Use the target language's established term for an organisation's style standard (German 'Hausstil', Turkish 'kurumsal stil'), or keep the English where local professionals do. | \"house rules\" = an organisation's standing working conventions - never the rules of a household. | \"in-house\" = done inside the organisation itself - never inside a building or dwelling. | \"design house / fashion house / publishing house\" = a company or atelier - never a physical building. | \"powerhouse\" = an idiom for something exceptionally capable - never a power station or building. | \"house reader rule\" = this codebase's own parser convention (never throws, null on bad input) - 'house' means the project, not a home.\nReturn ONLY the JSON matching the given schema - one translation per input id, in the same order.",
